course: dictionary
lesson: 10907
title: Words for wide views
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A vista is a wide view, often a beautiful one that you can see far across. Look at the clues in this sentence: «Connor hands in an essay about the breathtaking vistas of the Mediterranean.» Breathtaking means amazing, so vistas must be lovely views of the sea and the land.
explain: A vista can also be a view seen between things, like hills, trees or buildings. If a road bends and opens out, you might see a vista of the valley below. Ask yourself: what would fit here? A view, or a chance? The sentence tells you which.
explain: The word has a formal meaning too. «Reading opens new vistas» means reading gives you new chances and possibilities for the future. The clue is opens: a door opens onto new places, so new vistas are new possibilities. Try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: A vista is a beautiful, wide view, especially a long one.
fact: «A stunning vista» means an amazing view.
fact: «Sweeping vistas» are wide views across valleys, coasts or hills.
fact: In a formal sense, «new vistas» means new chances or possibilities.
fact: Vista is a noun, and its plural is vistas.
exercise: Connor writes about «the breathtaking vistas of the Mediterranean». What does vistas mean here? | Hot drinks | Small rooms by the sea | *Beautiful, wide views | Long, boring lessons
exercise: «Learning a language can open up new vistas.» What does vistas mean here? | *Chances and possibilities for the future | Angry phone calls | Cold winter weather | Lost keys
exercise: Which word in «The hikers stopped to admire the vista from the hilltop» names the view? | hikers | stopped | hilltop | *vista
test: «From the tower we saw a vista of rooftops and trees.» What is a vista here? | A map of the town | A tall tower | *A wide view over rooftops and trees | A narrow street
test: «Her new job would open up vistas she had never imagined.» What does vistas mean? | Windows in an old house | Long, quiet holidays | Views of faraway mountains | *New possibilities and chances
exam: Which sentence uses vista in the formal sense? | «The sky was full of stars.» | «The vista from the cliff was stunning.» | «We walked along the sweeping beach.» | *«Learning to code can open up new vistas.»
